874 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 3771 0 R
/E (Complex, Overlapping Regulations: Multiple authorities often result in conflicting directions, red tape, and delays in decision-making. For instance, private universities must comply with requirements from UGC, AICTE, and state councils, leading to confusion and inefficiency.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
875 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 3772 0 R
/E (Quality and Accreditation Gaps: More than half of Indian universities lack NAAC accreditation, contributing to the prevalence of "degree mills" and institutions with poor teaching standards.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
876 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 3773 0 R
/E (Curricular Stagnation: Excessive regulation leads to inflexible, outdated curricula, making graduates less employable \(as reported by the Economic Survey, nearly half of Indian graduates are not considered employable\).)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
877 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 3774 0 R
/E (Bureaucratic Inertia: Difficulty in launching new courses, updating syllabi, or collaborating with industry due to cumbersome approval processes.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
878 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 3775 0 R
/E (Equity and Access: Rigid regulatory mechanisms can exacerbate regional, socioeconomic, caste, and gender gaps in access to higher education.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
879 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 3776 0 R
/E (Politicisation: Centralized regulation can lead to political interference, as seen in appointment processes for academic leaders)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
